 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs to t1..tN
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Replaces: §stable-tab-keys (2026-09-02: "a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`)"). The maintainer overrode it on 2026-10-23.
+- Decision: keys stay contiguous. `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs, in their current order, to `t1, t2, …, tN` and resets `next` to N+1, so the next opened tab is `t(N+1)` with no gap or collision. The URL never shows gaps (`t1,t2`, not `t1,t3`).
+- Forbidden: leaving a gap after a close, or letting `next` drift from N+1 after a close.
+- Reason: the maintainer wants `?tabs=` to stay contiguous. Known cost, accepted by the maintainer: the key is also the React key, so the tabs to the right of a closed tab get new keys, remount, and lose scroll position and unsaved form input (the 2026-08-29 incident behind the old decision). Nothing else in this module refers to a tab by key (no active-tab pointer, no per-tab state maps), so `next` is the only key-derived state that has to be reset.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "close renumbers a middle tab and keeps order", "next open after close continues from the remaining count")
